Pesticide exposure
Social & Environmental
Miosis/blurred vision, Excessive salivation/sweating, Muscle fasciculations, Depressed AChE levels

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: SEV-036
Reason for Visit: Acute toxicological crisis following accidental ingestion/dermal exposure to an organophosphate agricultural chemical.
Location: Rural farming property
Present at Visit: Patient, farm manager
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a farm worker who suddenly collapsed while mixing chemicals, now presenting with profound vomiting, pinpoint pupils, and extreme difficulty breathing. Urgent assessment was required to diagnose acute cholinergic crisis and administer life-saving antidotes prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I can't see properly, my chest is so tight, and my stomach is tearing apart."
History of Present Illness: The farm manager reports the patient was mixing a concentrated insecticide approximately 45 minutes ago when a hose ruptured, splashing the chemical over the patient's torso and face. Key symptoms reflect a severe cholinergic toxidrome. The patient is experiencing profuse sweating, excessive salivation (drooling), and severe blurred vision. They report agonizing abdominal cramping accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea. The patient complains of profound weakness, and the manager notes muscle twitching (fasciculations). There is a distinct garlic-like odor on the patient's clothing and breath. The patient is becoming increasingly agitated and confused.
Functional Status: Normally independent; currently incapacitated by toxicological crisis
Social/Safety Concerns: Ongoing dermal absorption of a lethal toxin, risk of respiratory arrest, risk of secondary exposure to responders

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 90/50 | HR: 45 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 32 | SpO2: 86% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Profusely diaphoretic, drooling, vomiting actively, visibly twitching, smelling strongly of garlic/solvents
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals extreme miosis (pinpoint pupils, <1mm) bilaterally. Generalized, prominent muscle fasciculations are visible across the chest and proximal limbs. Respiratory auscultation reveals severe bronchorrhea (audible gurgling) and diffuse, tight wheezing. Cardiovascular exam shows profound bradycardia. Diagnostic tetrad features are starkly present: miosis/blurred vision, excessive salivation/sweating, muscle fasciculations, and a near-certainty of severely depressed AChE levels pending hospital bloodwork.
Environmental Observations: Patient is lying outside the mixing shed; contaminated clothing is still partially on.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Pesticide exposure (Organophosphate Toxicity / Cholinergic Crisis) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials: 1. Acute Asthma/Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of profound bradycardia (rather than tachycardia) and the massive secretory state (salivation, diarrhea, pinpoint pupils), which point definitively to cholinergic excess.
2. Narcotic Overdose - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of profuse bronchorrhea, hyperactive bowels/vomiting, and muscle fasciculations, which are absent in opioid toxicity despite shared miosis.
3. Seizure Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The fasciculations being continuous, irregular twitches of individual muscle bundles rather than coordinated tonic-clonic movements, alongside the massive autonomic symptoms.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is in an acute, life-threatening cholinergic crisis (DUMBELS syndrome: Diarrhea, Urination, Miosis, Bronchorrhea/Bronchospasm, Emesis, Lacrimation, Salivation) directly resulting from massive organophosphate exposure. Deferring assessment would result in imminent death from airway occlusion (secretions/spasm) and severe bradycardia. The objective findings of profound bradycardia, hypoxia (SpO2 86%), and bronchorrhea completely justify the urgent after-hours MBS intervention to immediately commence decontamination, administer life-saving Atropine, and coordinate emergency retrieval.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate, aggressive removal of all contaminated clothing (clinician utilizing PPE). Administration of high-dose intramuscular Atropine (e.g., 2mg stat, repeated every 5-10 minutes until secretions dry and bradycardia resolves). Suctioning of airway if available. Urgent dispatch of an intensive care ambulance/aeromedical retrieval.
Positive Behavioral Change: By letting us cut off these clothes right now and washing your skin with the hose, you immediately stop the poison from entering your blood, which gives the antidote time to reverse the breathing problems.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Mandatory notification to workplace safety authorities (WorkSafe).
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to Emergency Department/Toxicologist. Patient will require Pralidoxime and ICU admission.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate priority transport to the nearest tertiary hospital. The patient requires continuous cardiac and respiratory monitoring. Escalate Atropine dosing rapidly if SpO2 drops further or heart rate falls below 40.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: This chemical causes massive overstimulation of the nervous system. The antidote (Atropine) will dry up the dangerous fluid in your lungs and speed up your heart, but you will need several days of intensive hospital care to fully clear the toxin.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not try to move. Do not swallow the saliva; let it drool out so you don't choke. The ambulance is arriving to take over your care.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The farm chemical has paralyzed the switch that turns off your nerves, causing your body to flood itself with fluids. The injection I gave you acts as an emergency brake on those nerves to save your airway.
Actionable Steps: Keep your head turned to the side to let the fluid drain. Try to take slow, shallow breaths. We have secured the chemical spill area.

